WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.114 --> 00:00:04.500
[mast creaks]
[Euron yells]

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.350
You just made yourself a target, right?

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.183
There's one guy there.

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:08.870
It's relatively easy to defend against.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.930
Just shoot him.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.410
Hi, my name is Evan Wilson.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.960
I'm a professor at the
US Naval War College,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.990
and I'm an expert in
18th-century naval warfare.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.730
Today I'll be looking at
naval warfare scenes in movies

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.738
and judging how real they are.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.970
So, if you're desperate

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.520
and you're being chased by someone

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.230
who you know is stronger than you,

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.440
you would throw things overboard

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.520
if you're trying to speed up.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.930
But the thing that's really
going to lighten the ship

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.070
is throwing the guns overboard.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.850
I mean, that's the
fastest way to lighten it.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.560
A 32-pound cannon or something like that

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.350
weighs 7,000 pounds.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.757
What they're throwing overboard is like --

00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:48.997
a single cannonball isn't going to make

00:00:48.997 --> 00:00:50.771
any difference in the speed of your ship.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.508
This is a maneuver that's
called club hauling.

00:01:02.508 --> 00:01:03.580
You drop an anchor off the side

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.760
in an attempt to rapidly turn the ship

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.000
in another direction.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.590
It did happen, but you
did it in an emergency,

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.600
when you were trying
to extricate your ship

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.910
from a very dangerous situation,

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.860
rather than as a tactic in battle usually.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.140
You could surprise the Black Pearl,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.400
but it's going to take many minutes

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.130
for this maneuver to happen.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.030
I mean, it is a surprise,
'cause it's really dumb.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.470
So, that's chain shot.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:33.730
That's a real thing.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:35.471
It's either an antipersonnel thing,

00:01:35.471 --> 00:01:37.838
'cause it's going to spin,

00:01:37.838 --> 00:01:39.460
or you fire it at the rigging

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.580
in the hopes of cutting
ropes as it spins through.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.610
I'd be surprised if you
had a one-shot thing

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.120
that brings the mast down like that.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.140
A good naval gun crew that's
got a lot of men on it

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.170
can load and fire a cannon in a minute,

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.430
but you probably couldn't
sustain that for very long.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.910
It looks like there are, like,
eight people on this ship,

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:01.760
like I said, so, you know,
they could fire one gun.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.443
But not all of them.
You need a lot of men.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.220
It's a great way to
make yourself a target.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.750
Probably much more likely to
end up in between the ships,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.770
which is a great way to
get crushed to death.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:17.720
It's very cinematic, it's very evocative,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.660
and it looks like sort of a
Tarzan-esque thing, I get that,

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.830
but you're asking for trouble.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.000
And I think when people are firing at you,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.450
doing that is not smart.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.640
One of the things you don't see here

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.120
is that it's actually really hard

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.160
to keep two ships right
next to each other.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.820
We saw these ships moving
in opposite directions.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.490
Something would have
needed to have happened

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.570
to keep them near each other.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.330
Right now this is just, very conveniently,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:44.970
I guess they've anchored
next to each other

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.200
so that they can duke it out.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.180
Like, in the "Pirates" universe,
this is a 10 out of 10.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.430
It's about as accurate as they ever get.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.921
In reality, it's like a,

00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:54.075
I don't know, 4 out of 10.

00:02:54.075 --> 00:02:57.940
French officer: This is
your last warning! Stop now!

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.330
They showed a couple shots there

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.300
of the crowded deck of what
we know is a British frigate.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.570
That's much more accurate.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.671
I mean, these ships are going
to be crawling with men.

00:03:06.671 --> 00:03:09.296
Jack: Fire!
[cannons boom]

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.090
So, that's a pretty risky strategy,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.430
to try to knock down the
mainmast of the enemy ship,

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.190
because masts are hard to hit.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.480
But firing into the
rigging was a real tactic,

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.450
because the masts are
held up by the rigging.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.660
So the reason you'd want to
knock the enemy mast down

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.920
is to disable his ship.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.360
And then he couldn't chase you anymore,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.510
and then you could just leave.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.180
Often the British practiced
firing hard and low

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.350
into enemy hulls, into the enemy ship,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.000
with the idea of killing men.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.900
Not to sink the ship.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.780
It's really hard to sink a
wooden ship. Wood floats.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:45.690
Instead, what you're doing is

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.060
you're trying to kill the crew.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.970
The knock-on effect of that is that

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.130
you can either board them and take them,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.580
or they'll be so disabled and so damaged

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.820
that they will surrender to you

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.254
and then you can go take possession.

00:03:56.254 --> 00:03:58.604
Officer: Put out the boarding plank!

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.200
Those are ropes that they're throwing over

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.820
to try to get the ships to stay together,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.410
to solve that problem of
the ships moving apart.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:07.760
It's really hard to get two ships

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.320
to actually get close
enough together to board.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.105
Ships have something called a tumblehome.

00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:15.000
The hull is shaped so that

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.870
the sides aren't straight up and down.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.580
The sides actually turn in a little bit.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:20.580
Which means that when two
ships are next to each other,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.530
they both have tumblehomes
that are going this way,

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.470
which means that to cross
from one deck to the other

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.880
is actually a long way.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.940
The challenge of actually
getting two ships

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:31.460
next to each other to board them is real.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.683
They do a good job in this movie

00:04:32.683 --> 00:04:34.660
of showing you that
there are different ways

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:35.860
that you can go about doing it.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.620
I mean, they got almost everything right.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.073
This is a 10 out of 10.

00:04:39.073 --> 00:04:42.752
[Euron yells]
[cables creak]

00:04:42.752 --> 00:04:44.820
[bowsprit thuds]

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.180
That's supposed to be
sort of the bowsprit.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.320
That's the mast that sticks
out the front of the ship.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.430
That's really essential

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.050
to hold up most of the rigging of a ship.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:56.370
If you were to put elaborate
metal claws on the front of it,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.630
you'd, first of all,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.700
probably not be able to
sail the ship very well;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.960
secondly, it'd be very heavy
on the front of your ship

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.320
and cause your ship to go like this.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.070
You can run your ship into the other ship

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.900
and then board it from there.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.860
But you do that by just
clambering over your own bowsprit.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:13.960
You don't do it by staging an
elaborate entrance in which,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.200
once again, just like with
the Tarzan rope thing,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.840
you just made yourself a target, right?

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.580
I mean, they stand there
shocked that it's Euron,

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:20.764
but, like, just shoot him.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:25.860
Flaming arrows?

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:29.040
I would say no, that's not
something that would work.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.390
Mainly because to make
a flaming arrow work,

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.440
you probably have to
light it on your own ship,

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.970
and fire is by far the
biggest threat to ships.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:40.100
Wood, canvas, pitch, tar, this
stuff is really flammable.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.950
Firing flaming arrows
at someone else's ship

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.000
is much more likely to
set your own ship on fire

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.600
than to set the other guy's ship on fire.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.770
The fact that they correctly identify

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:52.245
that one of the greatest fears
for a fleet would be fire

00:05:52.245 --> 00:05:54.520
and that would be the thing
that would make Euron scary

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.321
I think is accurate.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.460
The two ships are about to be on fire.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.690
That would be very bad.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.240
That would be catastrophic.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.890
You would be much more
concerned about the fire

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.610
than about whatever the enemy's doing.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:15.220
Would you keep fighting in a storm?

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.440
The answer is, it would affect tactics,

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:19.480
but it wouldn't necessarily
keep you from fighting.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.600
There's a famous example
of two British frigates

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.920
chasing a much larger French ship in 1797.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.160
And normally, if the
weather had been calm,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.460
the two frigates probably
would have run away

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:32.880
from the bigger French ship.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.650
But because of the storm was so bad,

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.210
the French ship couldn't
open its lower gunports,

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.100
because the water would've
come in too low, right?

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.100
So the French ship was basically

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.850
half as powerful as it would've been.

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:53.786
I don't feel like I need to explain that,

00:06:53.786 --> 00:06:55.600
but I can, if you want.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.830
What's the name of the carnival ride?

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.130
That's what -- they clearly
saw that at a carnival

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.505
and they were like,
"Let's make that happen."

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:09.570
The guy swings like Tarzan, comes around,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.530
fires a gun, and then
gets shot in the face.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:12.555
Because of course he would.

00:07:12.555 --> 00:07:13.780
'Cause you see a guy
swinging, and you're like,

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.070
"I'm gonna shoot you now."

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.500
Boarding is risky, right?

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.691
When you board another ship,

00:07:17.691 --> 00:07:18.920
you need to be pretty confident

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.410
that you are superior to the
enemy that you're boarding.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.850
Because if you board an enemy
ship and it goes poorly,

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.130
then you might lose your own ship.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:29.030
Whereas if you hadn't bothered

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:30.140
to board them in the first place,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:31.750
you probably wouldn't get captured.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:32.780
And you'd be much more likely

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.920
to just obliterate them at a distance,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.427
and then once they surrender,
take over the ship and say,

00:07:37.427 --> 00:07:38.270
"Well, that was better."

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:41.070
A lot of movies have boarding in them

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.602
because it's very cinematic

00:07:42.602 --> 00:07:44.326
and it's a lot more interesting
than watching two ships

00:07:44.326 --> 00:07:45.940
just fire at each other for a long time.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:47.090
That's a 1.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:49.410
War at sea is not a carnival ride.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.660
That's just ...

00:08:00.337 --> 00:08:01.410
Look at the way that the wind

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:05.750
is blowing the sails of the
British ship from behind

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.360
and the sails of the other
ship from that direction.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.170
I mean, that's a really
good illustration of,

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.400
the wind doesn't work like that.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.859
The wind has been
disengaged from this scene

00:08:14.859 --> 00:08:17.150
so that these ships can
turn however they like.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.733
A lot of the times in
the age of sail clips

00:08:18.733 --> 00:08:19.750
that we've been seeing,

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.325
they're turning the
ship's wheel kind of like

00:08:21.325 --> 00:08:22.420
you're just sort of spinning it,

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.530
like, "Ah, just gonna throw
it this way and spin it."

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:25.930
Usually it took two guys.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:29.320
It would take two people there
to really work the wheel.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.350
'Cause you're turning a really
large piece of wood in water.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:35.730
So you turn the wheel,
and that that moves ropes

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.930
that are connected to the tiller

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:37.890
that are connected to the rudder,

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.660
and the rudder does steer the ship.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:42.060
So you do need to spin the wheel,

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:45.020
but often these ships
are turning on a dime

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.450
with nothing happening in the sails,

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.380
and you're expected to believe
that that's how it worked.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.160
So, you would turn the wheel to do that,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.100
but you'd also do 1,000 other things,

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.610
and the wind would have to be just right,

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.351
in the right place, and
it would take a while.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.870
These ships are passing
each other very quickly.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.690
I mean, the combined speed
there is, like, I don't know,

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.110
15 miles an hour.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.400
The number of seconds you'd probably have

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:13.960
in which the enemy ship

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:16.850
was in just the right
position to fire on it

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.240
would be very few.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.140
And so you'd end up with them just,

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:22.310
you know, you'd do a little bit of damage,

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:23.470
and then the ship would just sail on.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.770
You could double up on
an enemy ship like that,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.120
and there are famous examples of it.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.810
At the Battle of the Nile in 1798,

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.200
the British do this to the French.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.450
It's a great way to do a lot of damage,

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:34.130
'cause it forces the enemy
to fire on both sides,

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:37.088
to figure out which way to concentrate.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.520
They do a good job actually
here of showing the splinters.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.230
And the one realistic thing
about this clip is that

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.110
the air gets filled with splinters,

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.651
and splinters were the real danger.

00:09:54.651 --> 00:09:56.151
That was what hurt you.

00:09:56.151 --> 00:09:58.350
You're very unlikely to be
hit by an actual cannonball.

00:09:58.350 --> 00:09:59.183
It happened, of course,

00:09:59.183 --> 00:10:01.220
but the splinters are the thing here.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:04.130
Like, these things are
coming at bullet speeds.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.380
There are famous examples
of ships exploding.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:10.220
They're rare, but
certainly they did happen.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.210
The magazine is where
you store the powder.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:14.890
If the magazine catches fire,

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.450
then you'd see a major explosion.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.620
An iron cannon ball doesn't bring with it

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:20.810
any explosive power.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.030
There's nothing that goes boom there,

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:23.780
it's just a solid iron ball.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:26.040
So it's unlikely that an enemy shot

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.820
is going to cause a magazine to explode.

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:30.270
It's not something you
could really aim for.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.570
It's much more likely that
some fire got started on board

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:34.670
that then spread to the magazine.

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:37.280
Because, remember, all the
guns on that ship need sparks

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:40.280
in order to ignite the
powder to get them to fire,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.307
so there's a lot of flames

00:10:42.307 --> 00:10:44.223
in and around powder on board a ship.

00:10:44.223 --> 00:10:46.382
And if something goes wrong,
you could have that happen.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:53.335
Wood floats. Right?

00:10:53.335 --> 00:10:54.940
Wood doesn't take a nosedive and be like,

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:57.290
"Well, I'm done floating now. See ya."

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:58.460
There'd be wreckage everywhere,

00:10:58.460 --> 00:10:59.820
and all that wreckage would float.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.365
You'd end up with water
coming in the hull,

00:11:01.365 --> 00:11:03.130
and it would sink slowly over time.

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.130
Certainly not after a single pass

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.110
from two ships sailing
in the opposite direction

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.420
on a magical wind.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:10.850
So this clip is a 2,

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:14.400
mainly because of the splinters,
and ships did explode.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.825
But all the sailing stuff is ludicrous.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.120
Yeah, I don't know what to
say about that. [laughs]

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.594
What happens when a ship goes downhill?

00:11:27.594 --> 00:11:29.094
I don't know.

00:11:29.094 --> 00:11:31.390
I'd be much more concerned about the wave

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.410
and the curvature of the earth
there than the enemy ships,

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.627
but that's a fantasy
element we can leave aside.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.268
Rowing a ship using human power

00:11:44.268 --> 00:11:45.801
is pretty inefficient.

00:11:45.801 --> 00:11:48.590
The way to get your ship going fast enough

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.200
to do any damage with the ram

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.050
means that the rest of the
ship needs to be pretty light,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:53.350
and you need a lot of rowers.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.460
They've done some testing on this.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:57.210
The answer is, an untrained crew

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.340
could get a trireme going maybe 9 knots,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:02.090
which is like a jog.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.370
It's like 8 miles an hour, right?

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.390
So, yeah, it's not that fast.

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:08.410
The way that they're
moving relatively slowly

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.900
as they approach the ship is, meh, sure.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.149
And then it's sort of
accelerated at the end,

00:12:13.149 --> 00:12:14.920
and then the ship bent.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.340
You could probably poke
a hole in the guy's ship

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.840
if you really were going
to try to ram the guy.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.700
And you would aim for the
middle of the other guy's ship,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.580
'cause that probably
would be the weakest part,

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.000
but you're unlikely to sink him.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.980
'Cause, again, wood floats.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:25.990
It's really hard to sink ships.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.350
What you're more likely to
do is just damage him enough

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:29.530
that you can board him.

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:31.900
But what doesn't work in this clip is that

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:35.800
the vessels that you
would expect to see here

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.680
are fair-weather ships, right?

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:39.960
So galleys, triremes, that sort of thing.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.040
These are not all-weather vessels

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.600
that are going to handle a big sea.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.760
These ships would be mainly
struggling to survive,

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:47.520
rather than able to fight much.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:48.580
Maybe it's a 5.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.655
Yeah, they had triremes, they
fought like this, sort of.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.470
Their boat is turning into more boats!

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:01.500
The division of the pirate
ship into small ships

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:03.780
to send them out to attack the small ship

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:04.900
doesn't make a ton of sense to me.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:08.780
You'd use the small boats on your big ship

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:11.460
to tow you places in a flat calm,

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.820
sometimes, if it were an emergency,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:15.710
but otherwise the little
boats that you've got

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:17.627
are not going to help do much.

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:26.480
A smaller, maneuverable ship

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.650
could theoretically
run away from big ships

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.140
in the right conditions.

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:31.460
The speed of a ship depends on

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:32.965
about 1,000 different factors.

00:13:32.965 --> 00:13:35.850
You're going to deal
with the wind direction,

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.208
the wind speed,

00:13:37.208 --> 00:13:38.560
which direction the ship is trying to go

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:39.760
relative to the wind.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:41.720
Moana and Maui are going
to be more maneuverable.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.070
They're going to be able to
change direction very quickly.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:47.920
I think for realism, you know, it's a 6.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:49.170
I mean, there are coconut pirates,

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.536
so let's not get too carried away.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.080
So, now's probably a good
time to talk about range.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:01.573
The guns that they're
firing in this period

00:14:01.573 --> 00:14:03.430
are not usually rifled, right,

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:05.430
so they have smooth bore barrels,

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.960
which means that the cannonball

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.410
is slightly smaller than the barrel.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.430
Which means that as it
fires down the barrel,

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:13.263
it's got windage,

00:14:13.263 --> 00:14:15.530
so it can bounce around
the barrel as it goes,

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:16.400
and then it goes off there.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.050
So they're not particularly accurate.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.180
Think of a musket as opposed to a rifle.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.820
That said, they can
throw a ball really far.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:26.470
So, the muzzle velocity
of a 32-pound cannon here

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:29.328
is, like, 1,500 feet per second,

00:14:29.328 --> 00:14:31.903
or I think Mach 1.3.

00:14:31.903 --> 00:14:33.840
It's going faster than the speed of sound.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:37.723
And so you could hit something

00:14:37.723 --> 00:14:39.200
at maybe a mile.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:42.220
Whether you could hit what
you were aiming at at a mile,

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.490
less clear, but at a
mile you could probably,

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:48.500
if you got the pitch just right
and you hit it on the roll,

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.818
you could get a ball to go that far.

00:14:49.818 --> 00:14:51.611
Unlike most movies that immediately

00:14:51.611 --> 00:14:53.830
shrink the battlefield down
to right next to each other,

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:55.607
it does a good job of
sort of showing that,

00:14:55.607 --> 00:14:58.050
yeah, you could actually open
up at a pretty good range

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.720
and expect maybe to hit something.

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:02.604
Ideally you'd want to be
within a quarter of a mile.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:14.560
You'd only do that in very short ranges.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:16.727
Ships are constantly moving, right?

00:15:16.727 --> 00:15:18.750
And they're moving
faster than you can swim.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:22.390
So the idea that you would be able to risk

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:25.010
swimming across to an enemy ship

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:28.010
and then when you board it
there'd be enough of you

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:30.700
to actually do something
with it is very unlikely.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.760
In a fleet battle like
this it's very rare.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:34.250
What they did at Trafalgar,

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.150
this lieutenant swam
across to a French ship

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:39.340
and then managed to
get ropes tossed to him

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.200
so that they could grapple
the ships together,

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:42.240
again, to board it.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:43.415
But it's within range of someone

00:15:43.415 --> 00:15:44.390
to be able to throw a rope,

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.440
not, like, a mile or whatever
the distance is here.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:49.280
Would you do it at that range? No.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.900
Would you open up guns at that range?

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:53.030
It looks pretty far to me,

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:54.500
but it's hard to judge distances here.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:56.763
So I'll say 7.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:04.270
That weird funnel of ships was strange.

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:05.820
There are a lot of collisions
that are about to happen

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:06.653
in that fleet right there.

00:16:06.653 --> 00:16:08.150
So you wouldn't get that
close to each other.

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:10.880
But they're basically trying
to form a line of battle.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.570
Forming a line of battle was a technique

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.450
developed in the middle
of the 17th century,

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:17.590
in the 1650s, roughly.

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:20.060
And it was a defensive technique.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.412
So you form ships in a line of battle

00:16:22.412 --> 00:16:24.640
one after the other, lined up like this.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:25.920
Remember that ships' guns,

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.560
the most powerful ones fire sideways,

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:29.780
but ships sail forward.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.120
You line up all the ships in your fleet,

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:33.920
one after the other, like this in a line,

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:35.800
so that the weakest part of every ship,

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:38.890
the bow and the stern, is facing a friend.

00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:41.440
The most dangerous part
is facing the bad guy.

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:44.480
You form a really
effective defensive barrier

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:45.720
that means that it's very difficult

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:47.000
for the enemy to do anything to you,

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.130
because you can keep them at bay

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:51.640
with your massive powerful
guns all lined up.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:53.657
And that's called the line of battle,

00:16:53.657 --> 00:16:56.630
and it basically is the major fleet tactic

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:58.425
for the next 150 years.

00:16:58.425 --> 00:17:00.930
If you had a solid line of
battle that wouldn't get broken,

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.397
then chances are you're weren't
going to lose the battle.

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:11.189
That gun sort of gently
rolled back when it fired.

00:17:11.189 --> 00:17:13.540
Like, that's not nearly violent enough.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.750
7,000 pounds moving
backwards 10 to 11 feet,

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.440
you wouldn't really even
see it, it would be so fast.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:20.730
'Cause the thing that's
going out the front

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.010
is going out at Mach 1.3.

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:23.508
Now, that put out a huge amount of stress

00:17:23.508 --> 00:17:24.940
on the ship's side, 'cause it meant

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.870
that the bolts that held
it to the ship's side

00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:28.710
had 16 tons of force pulling them that way

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.200
every time you fired the gun.

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.030
So one of the things that
they learned over time is,

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.670
don't fire every single gun
on the ship at the same time.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:35.930
Do a rolling broadside

00:17:35.930 --> 00:17:38.900
so that you don't exert 16 tons of power

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:40.920
on every single bracket

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:42.260
on every single side of your ship.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:43.792
So bang, bang, bang,
bang, bang, bang, bang,

00:17:43.792 --> 00:17:45.770
not kaboom, right?

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:47.270
It wouldn't necessarily
tear the ship to pieces,

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:49.380
but it would certainly put
more stress on it than it would

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.530
if you sort of staggered
the firing a little bit.

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:55.780
To fire these cannon, put the
slow match on the touch hole.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:56.660
Well, all that's doing is

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:59.360
igniting a stream of powder fuse

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:01.218
that's going to go down the touch hole

00:18:01.218 --> 00:18:02.709
into the actual charge.

00:18:02.709 --> 00:18:06.160
And then sometime in the
next two or three seconds,

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.400
the cannon's going to fire.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:08.920
But I can't exactly predict it.

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:10.980
Which, again, makes aiming really hard,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:13.210
'cause the ship is moving
in all sorts of directions.

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:14.201
It's going forward,

00:18:14.201 --> 00:18:17.010
it's pitching and rolling and yawing,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:18.660
and all these other dimensions.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.668
They do a good job of actually showing

00:18:20.668 --> 00:18:21.550
slow match in this clip,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.950
but the guns don't recoil fast enough

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.430
and there's not enough
uncertainty about it.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:29.120
This is probably an 8.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:31.680
You don't normally see
lines of battle in movies,

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.570
and so credit where credit's due.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:37.140
If you take nothing else away
from this show, wood floats.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:38.056
If you enjoyed this video,

00:18:38.056 --> 00:18:40.006
click above to watch another one.

